                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="521-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Ningbo Alctron Electronics Co., Ltd. HRM-1</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Bidirectional Ribbon Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The HRM-1 is an inexpensive large-format ribbon microphone, modeled after AEA&#8217;s classic <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/AEA/R84">R84</a>. The ribbon measures 50mm x 5mm and is 2 microns thick. </p><p>These mics are sold by Alctron and rebadged by numerous US and European vendors; the HRM-1 can be purchased as the Nady RSM-2, the T.Bone RB500 and the PPA R-1.</p><p>Most reviewers consider this mic (like all Chinese-made ribbons) a great value for the money. Further, it responds well to modification; see the sidebar for modders who will restore clarity by removing blast filtering, improve frequency range and transient response by retensioning the ribbon and upgrading the transformer.</p><p>The mic ships in a soft padded box. The mic has an integrated yoke mount. Note that the XLR cable is hardwired to the microphone.</p><p>Although Alctron does not list the HRM-1 in its catalog any more, the mic appears to be identical to the RM-21.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Alctron/HRM-1">Ningbo Alctron Electronics Co., Ltd. HRM-1</a> is also known as: HRM1, RM-21.</p>
